# Intersession communication · engineering analysis

**Recommendation.** Keep the Full pack’s signed project journal as the record of work. A second session on the same machine reads it directly. A session on another device can receive the work through an explicitly addressed Companion push. To confirm delivery, read the signed event from the receiving device. For two sessions using the same authoritative local team-state file, the named recipient can accept the existing task handoff. Cross-device journal sync carries work history. It does not transfer ownership in that separate team-state file.

## Decisions for the specification

| Requirement | Reuse decision | Work and readiness |
|---|---|---|
| Resume work locally, including while Companion is stopped | **Adopt** the pack’s registered project UUID, signed journal, CLI status and audit. **Adopt** the team handoff for explicit ownership transfer. | Local state readback already passed in independent processes. Handoff acceptance, stale revisions, restart catch-up and concurrent claims still need real coverage. A same-authority recipient needs the referenced files and Git revision. The handoff contract commits acceptance atomically to one local team-state file. Copying this file or a packet to B cannot change A’s owner. Cross-machine ownership transfer needs a separately specified authority protocol. [Handoff authority](../../../../../skills/process/agent-team-creator/references/task-handoff.md) |
| Open the same trusted history on both devices | **Adopt** the Full release's KBD event reader in Companion. All four pack crates in its workspace currently share one Git revision. Refresh the four pins together and regenerate the lockfile while retaining existing constraints. | First implementation dependency. Old reader at `cfbc262` rejects a legitimate co-operator event before networking starts. Full `v1.17.0` (`acd029107533c07a661a7e3ad90eb28834688afa`) defines and applies it. Compatibility with the complete writer history still needs proof. The pin diff also adds `rs-fsrs` to learner-model and removes the optional iroh-docs feature from storage-provider. Companion already owns an iroh-docs adapter. Verify its consuming manifests against the new release. |
| Tell local state from a remote observation | **Adapt** the existing Full CLI read paths. Preserve the JSON state object, audit array and newline-delimited watch stream used by existing callers. | When remote is explicitly selected and unavailable, status and audit silently return local data; watch exits. Both read handlers currently attempt local replay on any remote error. For interactive use, allow labeled fallback for an absent endpoint or connection refusal. Surface explicit denial, project mismatch, malformed or incompatible responses as errors when a remote read was requested. Verify these with a populated local store so cached success cannot hide rejection. Use stderr for diagnostics. For an automated delivery check require a receiver-only read that fails when B is unreachable, initially via B’s existing Unix-socket API. Recommend a disconnected status and local catch-up for watch with event-ID deduplication. Never let a local fallback count as peer confirmation. |
| Send only to the chosen peer and prove application | **Adapt** the Companion’s existing iroh transport, signed push service, and Loro import. Keep the current two-operator fixture and a third unpaired node. | A transport acknowledgment confirms admission of a frame. Observe B’s audit, matching signature and integrity hash, advancing frontier, and regenerated waypoint to confirm application. Require the evidence to survive restarting B. Endpoint pairing and project device enrollment are distinct checks. A schema/authorization rejection must surface. |
| Handle disconnect and retry | **Adopt** existing command IDs, push IDs and persisted receipts. Classify local commit, uncertain reply, transport delivery and remote application separately. | The typed CLI already carries `committedLocally` and `remoteStatusUnknown` on its local fallback path. A successful replay of the same signed push must return the same receipt and leave one event. A saved failed push also replays its prior result: after resolving the failure, reconcile the original command ID and create a fresh push request when delivery is still needed. Exercise interruption before send, after commit and before the caller reads the response. |
| Share realistic project histories | **Adopt** the existing Loro update mechanism. Keep the same journal format for both modes. | Current export uses all updates; Companion serializes those bytes into a JSON payload and enforces a 16 MiB encoded peer-frame limit. At this inspection the Full project’s stored document was 4,897,496 bytes and Companion’s was 15,931 bytes. Neither disk size is a wire measurement. At the execution boundary measure the actual frame size using a disposable representative history, including near the limit. If it exceeds the bound, preserve the bound and use bounded authenticated batches or receiver-relative deltas with resumable baseline transfer. Confirm the final audit and frontier. |

### When the journal advances but the checkout stays stale

Companion imports the signed history before refreshing the checkout’s waypoint. A refresh error is logged and the import still returns success. The refresh is attempted only when new events were inserted, so an identical retry cannot be relied on to repair that waypoint. For Spec, require a recipient-side comparison of journal and projection revisions and a supported way to regenerate a stale projection from the durable history. Verify recovery after a temporary filesystem write failure and confirm the command appears exactly once. This uses the runtime’s existing projection comparison and writer. [Import path](/Volumes/T7/Projects/prometheus/prometheus-companion/crates/sovereign-sync/src/kbd_control.rs)

## How to prove the result

1. **Full only.** Install with no Companion endpoint. Session A commits and exits; B reads the signed event and next task. For ownership acceptance, both sessions address the very same authoritative team-state file. Read it independently after accept, unchanged replay and stale rejection. Check the next authorized KBD update separately. Replay and stale-owner cases must leave the expected owner and revision. Restart a watcher between commits, then check for gaps and duplicate effects. Preserve standalone installation, KBD doctor and signed audit checks.
2. **Version pair.** Use the installed writer to enroll a second operator, share the trusted baseline between independent test stores, then open both stores through the rebuilt Companion. Check the event and the enrolled keys. The previously supported journal and the new co-operator history must both open. A binary that encounters an unsupported event must preserve the history and identify the incompatible reader.
3. **Peer delivery.** Run Companion’s existing real-process verifier. B must initially be stale. Send the signed update explicitly to B, correlate audit and SSE with A's event, compare frontiers, restart B, then retry. Independently exercise a rejected target, a paired but unauthorized signer, a revoked device, and a wrong project. Each rejection must leave the receiver’s journal unchanged. Send the same command while disconnected, then reconnect and verify eventual delivery once.
4. **Network and scale.** A passing three-process test establishes interoperability on one host. Use two physical hosts on the expected LAN to claim cross-machine discovery, with distinct identities and journals. Include a realistically sized offline backlog before claiming coverage of this Full project. Use only disposable credentials and project data in the test.

The original peer test, schema rejection, pack continuity, disposable service recovery and standalone read/write results remain the baseline. No dependency was upgraded and no post-upgrade test was run during analysis. The nine standalone doctor failures and 130 existing Full OpenSpec failures remain separately visible. A successful peer run cannot clear those suites. [Assessment and receipts](assessment.md)

## Alternatives and constraints

A separate message broker would introduce another service and another record to reconcile when offline. A Git handoff or shared transcript alone cannot prove a recipient applied a signed event. A transport-level acknowledgment alone cannot show that B parsed the writer’s schema. The existing project mechanisms cover those responsibilities with fewer dependencies. Direct conversational messaging beyond work updates and accepted task handoffs remains a separate product question.

The verifier currently provisions an offline copy of a trusted baseline. Specify a supported bootstrap procedure before calling a new-device installation self-service. The behavior of physical LAN discovery and the encoded size of a representative backlog are open measurements. The local workspace manifest, lockfile and peer verifier were untracked in the Companion checkout during this inspection; preserving the narrowly reviewed files will be necessary for reproducible release builds.

Research used the established Rust stack and the checked-in or working-tree sources, upstream repositories, Context7 API documentation and the registry records for the locked versions. The registry marks iroh 1.1.0 and Loro 1.13.9 as available and not yanked. Full release source, local diagnostics and the lockfile are the basis for the compatibility decision. Upstream docs support API capabilities only. [Release reader](https://github.com/Prometheus-AGS/prometheus-skill-system/blob/v1.17.0/substrate/kbd-runtime/src/lib.rs) · [iroh](https://github.com/n0-computer/iroh) · [Loro export/import](https://github.com/loro-dev/loro/blob/main/crates/loro/src/lib.rs) · [Machine-readable candidates](library-candidates.json)

The recalled team-memory incident describes tasks marked done while canonical state lagged. That informs the requirement to read B’s actual signed journal and separately inspect its waypoint. The refreshed recall recorded no open knowledge gaps. [Phase recall](prior-context.md)

**READY to spec.** The architecture and the first repair are identified. Define the supported reader/writer pair, read-source behavior, addressed delivery, bootstrap and acceptance cases in the child specification. Implementation and certification require the child’s adopted acceptance contract. End-to-end peer delivery remains open.

